<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · c. 250 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Gloria in excelsis Deo</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A very old Christian hymn of praise that begins with the song the angels sang to the shepherds at Jesus's birth.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; opening words from the Gospel of Luke; Latin translation traditionally attributed to Hilary of Poitiers</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (more than two hundred medieval chant melodies)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>early Christians; later the Catholic, Orthodox, Oriental Orthodox, Lutheran and Anglican churches</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Koine Greek, Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>2nd or 3rd century; Latin translation 4th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous hymn of the 2nd or 3rd century; Latin translation attributed to Hilary of Poitiers (c. 300–368))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloria-in-excelsis-deo">Gloria in excelsis Deo</a>&quot; (&quot;Glory to God in the highest&quot;) is a Christian hymn also known as the Greater Doxology, the Angelic Hymn or the Hymn of the Angels. It opens with the words the angels sing to the shepherds at the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>, and other verses were added very early to make a hymn of praise. The scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Flusser linked the verse in Luke with ancient Jewish liturgy. The hymn belongs to the psalmi idiotici, &quot;private psalms&quot; written by individuals in imitation of the biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a>, which were popular in the 2nd and 3rd centuries; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/te-deum">Te Deum</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron</a> are other surviving examples. In the 4th century it became part of morning prayers, and it is still recited in the Orthros service of the Byzantine Rite.</p><p>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation is traditionally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilary-of-poitiers">Hilary of Poitiers</a> (c. 300–368), who may have learned the hymn while in the East in 359–360. It belongs to the early Latin translations of scripture known as the Vetus Latina: the Vulgate was commissioned only in 382, and the hymn's Latin wording for &quot;the highest&quot; differs from the one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> later preferred.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a tradition recorded in the Liber Pontificalis credits Pope Telesphorus (128–139?) with its use at the Mass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day and Pope Symmachus (498–514) with its use on Sundays and the feasts of martyrs, at first only by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. Priests were later allowed to sing it, first only at Easter and on the day of their ordination; by the end of the 11th century they used it on Sundays and feasts outside Lent. After the 12th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advent">Advent</a> came to be treated as a penitential season like Lent, and the Gloria was left out of it. Today the Roman Rite sings or recites it at Mass after the Kyrie on Sundays outside Lent and Advent and on feasts. In the Byzantine Rite it is one of the high points of festal Matins, chanted while the Holy Doors stand open and every lamp and candle is lit; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Church</a> recites it as the &quot;Praise of the Angels&quot;, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> sings it every day at the end of the midnight office.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> kept it in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> of 1549, then moved it to the end of the communion service, where it stayed in the 1552 and 1662 books until the 20th century. Lutheran churches use it in the Divine Service.</p><p>The Gloria has been sung to a great many melodies: scholars have catalogued well over two hundred from the medieval church. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> &quot;farced&quot; Glorias added phrases, often in honour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, and some were still sung when the Roman Missal was revised under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> in 1570. Almost every polyphonic setting of the Mass includes it, and composers from Vivaldi, Handel and Bach to Poulenc, Rutter and Karl Jenkins have set it alone. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> &quot;Angels We Have Heard on High&quot; takes its refrain from its opening.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise. It begins with the angels' greeting at the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, then adores and thanks God, and turns to Christ, honoured as the Most High, with a plea to receive the prayers of the faithful. The mood is festive and exultant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Koine Greek</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Koine Greek</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Gloria in excelsis Deo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Glory to God in the highest</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">et in terra pax hominibus bonae voluntatis.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and on earth peace to men of good will.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Laudamus te.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We praise you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Benedicimus te.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We bless you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Adoramus te.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We adore you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Glorificamus te.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We glorify you.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Gratias agimus tibi propter magnam gloriam tuam.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We give you thanks for your great glory.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Domine Deus rex celestis Deus pater omnipotens.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord God, heavenly king, God the Father almighty.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Domine Fili unigenite Iesu Christe.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord, only-begotten Son, Jesus Christ.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Domine Deus Agnus Dei Filius Patris.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord God, Lamb of God, Son of the Father.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Qui tollis peccata mundi miserere nobis.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You who take away the sins of the world, have mercy on us.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Qui tollis peccata mundi suscipe deprecationem nostram.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You who take away the sins of the world, receive our plea.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Qui sedes ad dexteram Patris miserere nobis.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You who sit at the right hand of the Father, have mercy on us.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Quoniam Tu solus Sanctus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For you alone are holy.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Tu solus Dominus.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You alone are Lord.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Tu solus altissimus Iesu Christe.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You alone are most high, Jesus Christ.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Cum Sancto Spiritu in gloria Dei Patris. Amen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With the Holy Spirit, in the glory of God the Father. Amen.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: The Latin text of the Roman rite. Text from <a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Gloria_in_Excelsis_Deo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (1)</summary><ul><li>line 1.2: 'hominibus bonae voluntatis' may be 'men of good will' or 'men of (God's) good pleasure'.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
